{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In unaffordable LA, a whopping 23% of \ndowntown land is surface-level parking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots like these. Toronto, the second \nepicenter of Canada's housing shortage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has hundreds of lots sitting empty. Meanwhile, \nin the second least affordable city in the world,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sydney, much of the most valuable land is \nfilled with lush, comfortable villas, many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of which are used as second homes. Even in crowded \nLondon, where land is used much more efficiently,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "property owners are still letting tens of \nthousands of homes just sit empty rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than renting them out to the teachers and \nnurses that desperately need them. How is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this possible? And how can we ever solve the \nhousing crisis if land is used so inefficiently?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, according to hundreds of comments that I \nalways get on every housing crisis video that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I make, the solution is simple. It's something \ncalled Georgism: a land value tax. And amazingly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to a University of Chicago survey, \n98% of economists that had an opinion on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter agreed that a land value tax would be a \ngreat idea. From right-wing free market Nobel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economists like Milton Friedman to left-wing \ncounterparts like Joseph Stiglitz and Paul  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76560,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Krugman, it seems like everybody loves land value \ntaxes—at least in theory, because puzzlingly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost no major city has fully adopted this \napparently brilliant idea. So what's going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on? Are land value taxes actually only good in \ntheory, or is there something else holding them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back from solving the housing crisis? As always \non Money \u0026 Macro, we have done a deep dive into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the scientific literature and talked to experts \nlike the president of research at the Center  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108640,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for Land Economics in Texas, all to find out: can \nland value taxes solve the global housing crisis?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All right, to answer that question, we need to \ntalk about the popular land tax movement that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would change the history of the United States: \nGeorgism. Our story starts in 1860 when American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "journalist Henry George just could not make \nsense of what he saw in booming New York City.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand, New York was quickly becoming \nthe richest city in the world. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the middle of all that wealth, there was \ndesperate poverty everywhere, even more than in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147440,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far less developed rural areas. How could that be? \nCould the answer be land? In the undeveloped West,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land was plentiful. But everybody wanted to live \nin booming New York, driving up the value of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161680,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land, making New York's landowners exceptionally \nrich. But was that fair? Industrialists had built  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New York's factories. Developers had built \nits magnificent homes. The city council  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was paying for street lighting, police, and \nsanitation. And meanwhile, the landowners,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they simply sat back watching the land value \ngo up, and they occasionally increased rent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In his 1879 book, Progress and Poverty, \nGeorge theorized that the reason that you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see poverty persist in some of the richest \ncities on Earth is simply because the gains  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from economic growth go not to those who \nproduce them, but rather to landowners.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The book became an absolute sensation, selling \nmore copies in America in the 1890s than any book  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except the Bible. George's thesis spawned a global \nmovement based on a radical yet elegant principle:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you should own 100% of the value created by \nyour own labor and capital, but the unearned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rent of land and natural resources should \nbelong to society. There's a broader economic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "philosophy. It's not just like \"land value \ntax is cool.\" It's a generalized anti-monopoly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "philosophy. And more importantly, it serves \nas a third-way philosophy that critiques both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we would now call capitalism and what \nwe would now call socialism. And actually,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these ideas became very popular in politics \nas well, heavily influencing political parties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the Commonwealth Land Party in the United \nStates, the Single Tax League in South Australia,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the Justice Party in Denmark, which even \nmade it into government in the late 1950s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, after a while, the idea of taxing land to \nreduce inequality was eclipsed by the Progressive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Era. Progressives also wanted to reduce \ninequality, of course, but they proposed doing so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280720,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a mixture of progressive income taxes and \ntough monopoly-busting antitrust laws. Meanwhile,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to the invention of the automobile, a \nlot of new land became available for housing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would quickly become the famous suburbs. \nArguably, so much new desirable land made housing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much more affordable in the 1950s and '60s. So, \nof course, George's land value tax idea became far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308000,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less popular, even if it never completely left the \neconomic debate. But now that suburbs have stopped  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding due to restrictive zoning regulations, \nthe fight for taxing land is actually gaining  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum again, with several states considering \nland value taxes to solve their housing crisis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of these years later, can \nGeorge's radical idea still work? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334720,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economists seem to think so. So why do 98% of \neconomists love land value taxes? The answer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is probably that a land value tax achieves \nsomething almost no other tax can: it seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348960,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both universally fair and highly efficient. Why is \nit fair? Because humans did not create the land;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357200,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was already there. Why should some humans reap \na huge financial reward from land while others get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "none? On the other hand, if you build a building \nor a farm on the land, or if you work to mine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373120,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out the minerals underneath it, then surely you \nmay have profited from unjust labor conditions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but at least you put some effort into it \nyourself and thereby should get a share at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least of the rewards. On the other hand, just \nowning a piece of land clearly does not seem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the kind of thing that should determine \nwho is rich and who is poor. Importantly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this principle—just owning land making \nyou rich—seems unfair to people both on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the political left and right. So that's why \na land value tax seems fair to many people."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why is it also efficient? Well, \nbasically because unlike most taxes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415760,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land value taxes do not destroy the incentives for \neconomic activity. A tax on labor makes working  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra hours less attractive. A wealth tax might \nlead some millionaires to move their businesses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430000,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abroad. But a land tax—wow, since the amount of \nland is fixed, you will not end up with less land  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you tax it. And therefore, where other taxes \nincentivize things that are bad for the economy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444640,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like less consumption or less work, land value \ntaxes should in theory incentivize good behavior,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is using land more efficiently to make \nback the tax. That is, with a sufficiently  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458480,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high land value tax, you would be crazy to \nlet land sit idle in the middle of Toronto, or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have surface-level parking spaces in central Los \nAngeles, or to let apartments sit empty in London."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to see fully how land value taxes can \nhelp solve the housing crisis, we need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contrast land value taxes with what they are \nmeant to replace: property taxes. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let's head to downtown Cincinnati. As you can see \nhere, the city is packed with big parking surfaces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even empty lots. But vacant land earns you \nno income, whereas land with a building could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be leased out or sold for a lot—probably for far \nmore than what you could earn by keeping it as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parking lot. So even without the land value tax, \nwhy would anyone in their right mind hold vacant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or underutilized land? After all, even if you \nare just betting on the value of land going up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would make sense to already build \nthere to collect rents from that building."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in our research, we found at least two \nmajor reasons why it does make sense to not use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the land properly. First, there's the so-called \noption value of the land. Imagine that you own a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541600,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot downtown. Offices are booming today, so you \ncould build an office block to profit from that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trend today. But remote work might kill office \ndemand in 10 years, or the area might become the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next hot residential district. Once the office \nbuilding goes up, you've made your bet for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next 50 years. And this uncertainty provides an \nincentive to wait and park cars in the meantime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the perfect opportunity comes along, \nrather than rushing to build there right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this effect is made far stronger because of \nreason number two, which is that current property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taxes directly disincentivize building. You see, \nproperty taxes in most cities are calculated as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "percentage of the assessed value of the entire \nproperty, which combines the value of the land  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the value of the building. And this means \nthat the more housing you build on your land,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the higher your tax bill will be. But if you \ndo not build, you will barely pay any taxes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if it's super valuable land. For example, \njust take a look at this street. Based on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612080,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assessment of the Center for Land Economics, \nthis local low-income housing unit pays around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$2 per square foot in taxes. Meanwhile, \nindustrial warehouses pay only $0.76, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this vacant lot pays just $0.17. And this \nmassive parking lot pays less than a cent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per square foot—exactly 200 times less tax than \nthe low-income housing unit. This difference in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taxes is so extreme that it may make total sense \nfor the parking lot owner to just keep this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultra-valuable land as a parking lot until the \nperfect opportunity comes along to build on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The basic idea of the land value tax \nis therefore to punish this behavior,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658560,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not reward it. In the most popular formulation of \nthe land value tax, it would essentially replace  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "property taxes with a split tax rate: a low rate \non the structures and a high rate on the land.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a land value tax basically exempts the \nbuilding portion of a conventional property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tax from taxation and increases the tax rate on \nthe land. And if you reduce the tax on property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough and raise the tax on land enough, then \naccording to the laws of supply and demand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something magical will happen that can change \nthe fate of thousands who cannot afford to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rent in major cities. American parking lots, \nCanadian vacant lots, empty London apartments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "empty Sydney villas on prime land—many of them \nwill all be replaced with affordable homes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such that regular working people can once \nagain live in these unaffordable cities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least in theory, because as we have seen, the \nidea of land value taxes has been popular for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decades. But if it's such a great idea, why do \nland value taxes never get implemented? I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not like land value taxes have never \nbeen tried before. Britain tried it in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early 20th century during the peak of \nthe Georgist movement. Pittsburgh ran  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a split-rate tax for most of the 20th \ncentury. And today, Denmark, Estonia,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and several Australian states collect land value \ntaxes. And just this year, Kentucky and Virginia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748160,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passed legislation allowing cities to implement \nsplit-rate taxes on both structures and land."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what can we learn from these \nexperiences? Well, unfortunately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less than you might hope because, well, there \nhaven't been many solid studies done on them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes, there are some studies and they do seem \npromising, linking land value taxes to increased  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "housing construction, lower vacancy rates, \nand reduced urban sprawl. But many of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studies suffer from a lack of reliable data, \nmeaning that we cannot know for sure. Still,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the limited evidence that we do have is, in my \nopinion, promising enough to say that land value  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taxes do indeed seem like more than just good in \ntheory. They probably work in practice as well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So then why aren't more places trying them? In \nour research, we kept coming across two major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practical problems that have prevented land value \ntaxes from being consistently implemented and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "replicated across the world. The first problem \nis a valuation problem. The basic idea of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land value tax is to tax land separately from \nthe structure and improvements on top of it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how do you consistently isolate the value \nof land from everything else? Because if we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply look at sales data, it doesn't tell \nus anything about what the buyers actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paid for. Did they pay for the land mostly \nor for the stuff that's on it? And this makes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land value taxes significantly more difficult to \nimplement than income taxes or value-added taxes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are just a simple percentage of \ntransaction values. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like wealth taxes, land value taxes \nforce governments to make complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculations to separate the value of the \nland from everything that's on top of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this complexity is precisely what \ndoomed Britain's attempt to implement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a land value tax through the famous 1910 \nPeople's Budget. You see, to implement it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872960,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government found it so difficult to \ncalculate the value of land of properties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that had rarely ever been sold that they forced \nlandowners to do it themselves or be fined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "£50. And this turned out to be a major mistake \nbecause it allowed landowners to fight the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tax in court. And after a legal battle that \ncost more than the entire tax helped collect,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the British state lost and gave \nup the dream of a land value tax."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the second problem that we consistently \ncame across is a political problem. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "middle-class voters usually have their wealth \nstored in their homes and by extension in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land underneath it. Land values of residential \nproperties have increased a lot during the last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decade, especially in major cities. So are \nmiddle-class homeowning voters holding back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land value taxes? Or is it that land value \ntaxes are just too difficult for governments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to implement? In other words, are \nland value taxes doomed to fail?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The good news is I don't think so. Yes, land \nis tricky to value in isolation from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structures on top of it. But today, with modern \ntechniques, it should be much easier than in early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "20th-century Britain. Take Denmark, one of the \nfew countries that currently has land value taxes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While it was not easy to set up, their Ministry of \nTaxation eventually was able to set up a massive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highly digitized public database to run \nautomated mass appraisals, re-evaluating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every piece of land in the country every 2 years. \nAnd if that sounds too expensive, countries can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always try to follow South Korea's example, \nwhose government uses so-called representative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plots of land as a benchmark to split land \nand property values in the entire country."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fundamentally, any data scientist will tell you \nit is a data quality issue. Like if you collect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough information on construction costs and you \ncollect enough information on sales and rents,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like there is any number of methods, ancient and \nmodern, that can get this out. In other words,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the data issue may have held back land \nvalue taxes in early 20th-century Britain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003120,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern techniques should make it \nvery doable for governments to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "replace property taxes with a split \nstructure and land value taxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, is it instead that middle-class \nhomeowners, a powerful voting block,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will inevitably block land value taxes \nin all democracies? Not necessarily,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because while a shift from property to land \nvalue taxes will produce some winners and losers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is not middle-class families who are likely \nto bear the burden. For example, in Cincinnati,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Center for Land Economics has modeled \nhow a shift from property to land value taxes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would change the final tax bill of different \ntypes of properties. The biggest losers by far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the owners of vacant lots and big parking \nsurfaces. Meanwhile, while the biggest winners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be renters in bigger buildings, crucially, \neven middle-class families in single-family homes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058480,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would still win from the proposal, making it \nmuch more likely that the proposal will pass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, actually, I think it's pretty likely \nthat, as the housing crisis continues,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we will see more and more states, cities, and \nperhaps even countries adopt land value taxes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077840,
    "dDurationMs": 8460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But will that be enough? Can land value taxes \nactually fix the housing crisis? Sadly, I do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086300,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not think so for two reasons. First, if we look \nat the parking issue, we can see that actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most cities that do not have these sprawling \nparking zones, like New York, Washington, and San  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098240,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Francisco, actually have some of the worst housing \ncrises. Meanwhile, in cities with a lot of vacant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106240,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land like Toronto, it turns out that a lot of \nthat land is actually held hostage by bureaucratic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "issues from the institution that should profit \nfrom the land value tax—the government itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, in Seoul, South Korea, where they do tax \ncommercial land separately from structures on it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I did see some of the predicted effects: \nalmost exclusively high-density homes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no surface-level parking, only \nunderground parking lots everywhere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost no vacant lots. I was really amazed at how \nefficiently they used their most valuable land.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet the inhabitants were still complaining \nabout a housing affordability crisis. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I do not think that land value taxes are a \nmagic bullet. They can absolutely help! But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to truly solve the housing crisis, I think \nland value taxes should be combined with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other measures that help to build more homes \nwhere they are needed, like public housing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building new cities if existing ones get too \npopular, and less restrictive zoning regulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But yeah, these are my takes. Let me \nknow if you also think that land value  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1180240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taxes could play an important role in \nfixing the housing affordability crisis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that it's not enough to fix it on its own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you want to dig deeper into the \nfascinating world of land value taxes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1193360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I highly recommend you check out the following \n3 articles from our advertising sponsor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist. First, for a general overview \nof the history of land value taxes and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1204800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practical problems of implementing them, I \nwould start with this article. Next, go check  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out their take on Detroit's move to be the first \nAmerican city with a land value tax. Finally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1218880,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finish with their take on why land value taxes are \nso popular, yet so rare. As these analyses show,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1227040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist delivers insights that truly \nhelp you see the bigger picture. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why I highly recommend you subscribe to The \nEconomist, which now offers an exclusive 35%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discount for Money \u0026 Macro viewers. Whether you \nprefer daily journalism in The Economist app,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1243360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or you're like me and enjoy catching up on \nthe global economy over the weekend with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a cup of coffee and the print edition, \nyou'll always stay on top of the latest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "global developments—and support journalism that \nvalues factual information, integrity, and an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1257760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent point of view. So don't miss out—click \nthe link in the description or top comment below,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263680,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or head over to economist.com/moneymacro \nto claim your exclusive 35% discount today."
    } ]
  } ]
}
